        "problem": "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}/y}.$",
        "worked_solution": "Notice that\n\n- $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}$ is defined for $x$ and $y,$ such that $y\\ne 0,$ and\n\n- ${e}^{g(x,y)}$ is defined if $g(x,y)$ is defined.\n\nAs a result, ${e}^{{x}^{2}/y}$ is defined for values of $x$ and $y$ that satisfy $y\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$",
